[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"RxJava Operators, Schedulers & Backpressure in Android (Java)"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Reactive Programming in Android allows you to handle asynchronous data streams cleanly. This tutorial covers the core concepts of RxJava: Operators for transforming data, Schedulers for threading, and Backpressure for handling fast data producers."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Common RxJava Operators"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Operators are methods that modify or manipulate the data emitted by an Observable before it reaches the Observer. Here is an example combining map, filter, and flatMap in a typical Android ViewModel setup."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"Disposable disposable = Observable.fromIterable(getUserList()) 
    .filter(user -> user.isActive()) 
    .map(user -> user.getName()) 
    .flatMap(name -> fetchUserDetailsFromName(name)) 
    .subscribe( 
        details -> Log.d("RxJava", "Details: " + details), 
        error -> Log.e("RxJava", "Error: " + error.getMessage()) 
    );"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Managing Threads with Schedulers"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"Schedulers determine which thread the Observable executes on. In Android, network or database operations must run on background threads, while UI updates must happen on the main thread. We use subscribeOn() and observeOn() to achieve this."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Schedulers.io(): Used for I/O-bound work like network requests, disk reading/writing."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Schedulers.computation(): Used for heavy CPU-bound computational work."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"AndroidSchedulers.mainThread(): Switches execution to the Android main UI thread."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"apiService.getData() 
    .subscribeOn(Schedulers.io()) 
    .observeOn(AndroidSchedulers.mainThread()) 
    .subscribe( 
        result -> showDataOnUI(result), 
        throwable -> handleError(throwable) 
    );"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Handling Backpressure"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"Backpressure occurs when an Observable emits items faster than an Observer can consume them. In RxJava 2 and 3, standard Observables do not support backpressure, but Flowable does. When using a Flowable, you must define a BackpressureStrategy."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"BackpressureStrategy.BUFFER: Buffers all items until the downstream consumes them (can cause OutOfMemoryError if too large)."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"BackpressureStrategy.DROP: Drops the most recent items if the downstream cannot keep up."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"BackpressureStrategy.LATEST: Keeps only the latest item, overwriting previous unconsumed items."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"Flowable.create(emitter -> { 
    for (int i = 0; i < 10000; i++) { 
        emitter.onNext(i); 
    } 
    emitter.onComplete(); 
}, BackpressureStrategy.BUFFER) 
    .subscribeOn(Schedulers.computation()) 
    .observeOn(AndroidSchedulers.mainThread()) 
    .subscribe( 
        item -> Log.d("Backpressure", "Consumed: " + item), 
        error -> Log.e("Backpressure", "Error: " + error.getMessage()) 
    );"},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"By correctly pairing Operators for data manipulation, Schedulers for thread management, and Flowables with Backpressure Strategies for high-frequency data, you can build responsive, memory-efficient Android applications."}]
